\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Effect size and practical significance.}
The pairwise $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}1.7\%$ invites the question of whether confusability matters in practice. Two considerations argue that it does. First, the pairwise analysis operates at fine granularity ($n = 1{,}953$ type pairs for Few-NERD), where most pairs have zero or near-zero confusion; the signal concentrates among the minority of pairs the model actually confuses, where Spearman $\rho$ rises to $0.27\text{--}0.31$ (\S\ref{sec:pairwise}). Second, at the batch level---the operationally relevant unit for prompt construction---BCS-controlled experiments produce accuracy gaps of 31.2 percentage points between low-BCS and high-BCS batches (Table~\ref{tab:bcs_controlled}), and this gap amplifies $3.4\times$ as the type pool grows from 16 to 80 relations. A small pairwise signal thus compounds into large practical effects when multiple confusable types co-occur.

\paragraph{Task-dependent error mechanisms.}
The NER--RE divergence on missed-entity rates (\S\ref{sec:multi_channel}) reveals that confusability operates through different mechanisms across tasks. In RE, where entity boundaries are given and only the relation label must be predicted, BCS exclusively drives type confusion: missed rates remain flat at 10--16\% regardless of confusability. In NER, missed rates are substantially higher (43--65\%) and vary non-monotonically with BCS, indicating that confusable type sets impair entity detection, not only type discrimination. A plausible explanation is that NER requires joint boundary-and-type decisions: when the candidate types for a span are semantically similar, the model may abstain from extraction rather than commit to an ambiguous label. This boundary--type coupling is absent in RE, where spans are pre-specified.

\paragraph{Embedding choice.}
Our SCS analysis uses two embedding spaces: Qwen3-32B hidden states (5{,}120-d) and all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384-d). On all type pairs, Qwen3 yields stronger overall correlation (Spearman $\rho = 0.168$ vs.\ $0.144$), reflecting the extraction model's own type representations. Among pairs with nonzero confusion, MiniLM yields a stronger signal ($\rho = 0.306$ vs.\ $0.272$). Neither embedding uniformly dominates. In practice, extraction-model embeddings are preferable when available, as they capture model-specific confusability; general-purpose encoders like MiniLM are a viable alternative when extraction-model representations are inaccessible.

\paragraph{Limitations and open questions.}
Several limitations bound the scope of our findings. All primary experiments use Qwen3-32B; preliminary LLaMA-3.1-8B results replicate the count-degradation pattern (recall: $0.70 \to 0.27$, per-batch correlation with Qwen3: $r = 0.72$), but full cross-model BCS-controlled validation is ongoing. Our within-dataset $2\!\times\!2$ factorial analysis on Few-NERD did not reach statistical significance ($z = -0.67$, $p = 0.50$), possibly due to BCS range compression at high type counts; the cross-scale $3.4\times$ amplification on FewRel remains the primary interaction evidence. We did not test intermediate schema sizes (e.g., 40 relations) between the 16- and 80-relation FewRel experiments, leaving the shape of the scaling curve underspecified. Finally, SCS/BCS relies on embedding cosine similarity; alternative confusability metrics---such as label entropy, ontological depth, or empirical confusion priors---may capture complementary dimensions of schema difficulty.

