\paragraph{Summary.}
We isolated schema-induced degradation into two channels---count-driven recall decline and confusability-driven type confusion---and showed these are cleanly separable in RE but interact in NER. Pairwise SCS is a partial but robust predictor, replicating across three tasks and two embedding spaces. We further identified a centrality confound on same-domain schemas and showed that merge amplification is the structural bottleneck for decompose-then-merge pipelines. Addressing this via cross-batch calibration or joint decoding is the key next step.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The pairwise $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}1.7\%$ is modest but concentrates among pairs the model actually confuses ($\rho = 0.27\text{--}0.31$). At the batch level, accuracy gaps reach 31.2\,pp and amplify $3.4\times$ from 16 to 80 relations---a small pairwise signal compounding into large effects when confusable types co-occur. The NER--RE divergence on missed rates reveals task-dependent mechanisms: in RE, BCS drives type confusion while detection stays flat (10--16\%); in NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic, suggesting confusable types impair entity detection itself through boundary--type coupling. On embedding choice, Qwen3 hidden states yield stronger overall correlation ($\rho = 0.168$ vs.\ MiniLM's $0.144$), while MiniLM is stronger among nonzero-confusion pairs ($\rho = 0.306$ vs.\ $0.272$); extraction-model embeddings are preferable when available. Preliminary LLaMA-3.1-8B results replicate Channel~1 (recall: $0.70 \to 0.27$; per-batch $r = 0.72$ vs.\ Qwen3); full cross-model validation is ongoing.

In sum, we isolated schema-induced degradation into count-driven recall decline and confusability-driven type confusion, showed these channels are separable in RE but interact in NER, identified a centrality confound on same-domain schemas, and demonstrated that merge amplification is the bottleneck for decompose-then-merge pipelines. Cross-batch calibration or joint decoding is the key next step.

